1
00:00:02,177 --> 00:00:03,177
[SPEAKER_02]: So, what's sense-restraint?

2
00:00:07,718 --> 00:00:24,481
[SPEAKER_02]: Sense-restraint... You've been saying, you've been saying, recently, that it's important for a lay person, even if they're not become a monk, they really want to progress in dhamma, sense-restraint is optional.

3
00:00:25,282 --> 00:00:27,542
[SPEAKER_02]: But I wonder if everyone knows what

4
00:00:31,270 --> 00:00:32,851
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the most obviously, it's the precepts.

5
00:00:33,492 --> 00:00:35,994
[SPEAKER_01]: So, five precepts, eight precepts.

6
00:00:37,355 --> 00:00:45,661
[SPEAKER_01]: But, eh... it needs to be taken on as... as I think I discussed that in a previous talk.

7
00:00:46,061 --> 00:00:50,965
[SPEAKER_01]: You take it on as your value precepts, but then you can obviously keep refining that value.

8
00:00:51,386 --> 00:00:55,709
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's where the... where the sense restraint... you can really benefit from it.

9
00:00:56,930 --> 00:00:58,031
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you can, eh...

10
00:00:59,130 --> 00:01:01,894
[SPEAKER_01]: Precepts in itself will not make a practice a dhamma.

11
00:01:04,317 --> 00:01:09,523
[SPEAKER_01]: So you can have absolutely unbroken precepts, but also never really make the effort to think about

12
00:01:10,972 --> 00:01:17,015
[SPEAKER_01]: Motivation behind your actions, whether it's rooted in desire or greed or aversion, but you just adhere to the precepts.

13
00:01:17,435 --> 00:01:24,979
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's still better than not keeping them at all, but in itself, it won't result in any understanding.

14
00:01:25,039 --> 00:01:30,322
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if it was so, then the Buddha would have said, all you need to do is be virtuous and you'll become an arahant.

15
00:01:31,983 --> 00:01:39,347
[SPEAKER_01]: So, dhamma... there is no dhamma without sīla, there is no dhamma without the precepts, but dhamma is not the precepts.

16
00:01:40,862 --> 00:01:52,966
[SPEAKER_01]: So, sense-restraint starts with the precepts, but then you can start to sort of train and practice understanding of it.

17
00:01:53,767 --> 00:01:56,908
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's not just you don't do certain things that are rooted in desire.

18
00:01:57,508 --> 00:02:01,189
[SPEAKER_01]: You also want to make the effort while you're not doing those things rooted in desire.

19
00:02:01,569 --> 00:02:06,571
[SPEAKER_01]: So, while you're restrained, you want to make the effort to understand why is that good?

20
00:02:07,351 --> 00:02:08,712
[SPEAKER_01]: Why is sense-restraint wholesome?

21
00:02:10,510 --> 00:02:31,565
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's what I mean, like, the precepts are obvious things you should not go below, but then, if you start following the principle of anything that's rooted in desire, anything that's rooted in aversion, anything that's rooted in distraction and delusion, is unwholesome.

22
00:02:32,565 --> 00:02:37,609
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, even some things seemingly wholesome externally, are actually unwholesome, because

23
00:02:39,366 --> 00:02:47,554
[SPEAKER_01]: They're determined as unwholesome, not based on the external perception of it, but based on whether they're rooted in greed, aversion and delusion, internally.

24
00:02:48,334 --> 00:02:50,076
[SPEAKER_02]: So, it's all about your motivation?

25
00:02:51,157 --> 00:03:00,925
[SPEAKER_01]: It's about, yeah... well, it's about you developing the... the sight of the motivation behind your actions, which is always there.

26
00:03:01,746 --> 00:03:05,229
[SPEAKER_02]: And... sort of discriminating, like...

27
00:03:07,262 --> 00:03:14,785
[SPEAKER_02]: Because I think, you know, some people... you're likely to take central restraint too far.

28
00:03:16,166 --> 00:03:16,686
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, of course.

29
00:03:16,726 --> 00:03:18,067
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, that's the natural response.

30
00:03:19,647 --> 00:03:20,788
[SPEAKER_01]: Completely unrestrained.

31
00:03:20,808 --> 00:03:22,368
[SPEAKER_02]: Keep my eyes downcast, don't look at anything.

32
00:03:22,408 --> 00:03:23,489
[SPEAKER_02]: And say no to everything.

33
00:03:25,070 --> 00:03:25,830
[SPEAKER_01]: No, that's what I mean.

34
00:03:25,850 --> 00:03:27,811
[SPEAKER_01]: The starting point is not saying no to everything.

35
00:03:27,851 --> 00:03:35,054
[SPEAKER_01]: The starting point is saying no to obviously unwholesome things, either against five precepts, or if you're taking on the eight precepts, against eight precepts.

36
00:03:35,915 --> 00:03:38,437
[SPEAKER_01]: If it's not against those things, it's fine.

37
00:03:40,239 --> 00:03:47,365
[SPEAKER_01]: But that doesn't mean you should be unclear in regard to either.

38
00:03:48,947 --> 00:03:52,490
[SPEAKER_01]: You need to have clarity why is this fine and why this isn't fine.

39
00:03:53,668 --> 00:03:57,791
[SPEAKER_01]: So, yes, you've taken it on as an external value of virtue must be developed.

40
00:03:57,831 --> 00:03:59,672
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't know why, but I have to start developing it.

41
00:03:59,913 --> 00:04:01,234
[SPEAKER_01]: That's a necessary first step.

42
00:04:01,694 --> 00:04:05,577
[SPEAKER_01]: But then, you also want to understand why it is necessary and to what extent it's necessary.

43
00:04:05,677 --> 00:04:09,420
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you might say central strength is taking that kind of...

44
00:04:10,340 --> 00:04:17,942
[SPEAKER_02]: Sort of mechanical, you know, just sort of thoughtlessly avoiding certain actions, is taking it into the realm of understanding.

45
00:04:18,363 --> 00:04:18,943
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly, yeah.

46
00:04:19,843 --> 00:04:31,387
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, as you said, it's quite normal to just... once you think, okay, so virtue is necessary, virtue is valuable, sense of strength is valuable, then you start restraining against everything, saying no to everything.

47
00:04:31,427 --> 00:04:31,627
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

48
00:04:32,187 --> 00:04:32,867
[SPEAKER_01]: I want to go for a walk.

49
00:04:32,907 --> 00:04:33,087
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

50
00:04:33,747 --> 00:04:34,388
[SPEAKER_01]: I want to sit down.

51
00:04:34,448 --> 00:04:34,608
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

52
00:04:34,948 --> 00:04:35,548
[SPEAKER_01]: I want to stand up.

53
00:04:35,588 --> 00:04:35,728
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

54
00:04:36,666 --> 00:04:36,826
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

55
00:04:36,926 --> 00:04:38,547
[SPEAKER_01]: And then you just wind yourself up completely.

56
00:04:38,727 --> 00:04:38,887
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

57
00:04:38,987 --> 00:04:39,508
[SPEAKER_02]: I remember people...

58
00:04:39,528 --> 00:04:40,688
[SPEAKER_02]: There's no wisdom there either.

59
00:04:41,008 --> 00:04:42,810
[SPEAKER_01]: Not more than if you just say yes to everything.

60
00:04:43,390 --> 00:04:48,713
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why I said self-mortification and sensuality are basically two ends of the same plane.

61
00:04:49,513 --> 00:04:59,299
[SPEAKER_02]: I remember hearing a story about a monk who was staying in a cave and some lay people came and pointed out the drawings on the cave walls.

62
00:04:59,879 --> 00:05:00,339
[SPEAKER_02]: Amazing.

63
00:05:00,760 --> 00:05:01,200
[SPEAKER_02]: And the monk

64
00:05:03,225 --> 00:05:16,949
[SPEAKER_02]: So, all you need to do to free yourself from suffering is look at the ground.

65
00:05:17,129 --> 00:05:18,229
[SPEAKER_01]: Just keep looking at the ground.

66
00:05:20,030 --> 00:05:22,971
[SPEAKER_01]: So, does that imply, if you look elsewhere, that's where the suffering is rooted?

67
00:05:23,011 --> 00:05:23,991
[SPEAKER_02]: It does imply that, doesn't it?

68
00:05:24,011 --> 00:05:24,271
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

69
00:05:25,912 --> 00:05:26,952
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's a problem with our view.

70
00:05:28,877 --> 00:05:33,980
[SPEAKER_02]: So, basically, sensory restraint really is about learning to find out where the problem is.

71
00:05:34,220 --> 00:05:34,400
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

72
00:05:35,061 --> 00:05:40,504
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the value of precepts now made even more personal, internalized, so to speak.

73
00:05:40,524 --> 00:05:47,467
[SPEAKER_01]: So, now you recognize the value of knowledge, the value of restraint, as opposed to non-value of lack of restraint.

74
00:05:48,148 --> 00:05:56,552
[SPEAKER_01]: You start to recognize why, when you avoid to look, taste, smell, touch, and so on, things out of desire, why is that better?

75
00:05:59,056 --> 00:06:00,257
[SPEAKER_01]: It won't be clear overnight.

76
00:06:00,297 --> 00:06:02,359
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not like a one... oh, yes, that's why.

77
00:06:02,919 --> 00:06:11,567
[SPEAKER_01]: It's something that needs to be sort of trained in, to sort of keep reapplying that value until it becomes clear in itself, because it is better.

78
00:06:12,728 --> 00:06:20,195
[SPEAKER_01]: But until you know for yourself why, you will be then adhering to your sīla, your virtue, your sense restraint as a... it will be affected to you.

79
00:06:20,755 --> 00:06:25,860
[SPEAKER_01]: It will be something you do on account of things you don't do, but either way there is no understanding in regard to it.

80
00:06:26,593 --> 00:06:29,334
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why we often talk about motivation behind the actions.

81
00:06:29,954 --> 00:06:30,855
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what determines.

82
00:06:31,375 --> 00:06:33,556
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, that's always like... even the precepts.

83
00:06:33,796 --> 00:06:43,700
[SPEAKER_01]: Breaking the precepts is bad, not because it's bad in itself, but it's bad because it's impossible to break the precepts without the motivation being rooted in greed, aversion, delusion.

84
00:06:45,020 --> 00:06:51,162
[SPEAKER_01]: So, there are certain things that if done, they can only be done either by greed, aversion or delusion.

85
00:06:51,182 --> 00:06:52,222
[SPEAKER_01]: Five precepts, not the eight.

86
00:06:52,342 --> 00:06:53,282
[SPEAKER_01]: Five precepts, yeah.

87
00:06:53,702 --> 00:06:56,203
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why that's always bad.

88
00:06:57,003 --> 00:07:03,045
[SPEAKER_01]: But... and that's why even if somebody who doesn't want to practice dhamma, should live by the five precepts.

89
00:07:04,153 --> 00:07:07,814
[SPEAKER_01]: Because those things are on the level of animal behavior, as the Buddha described.

90
00:07:08,315 --> 00:07:18,679
[SPEAKER_01]: But now, we are talking about somebody who wants to practice dhamma, not just be a virtuous person and living a life without any pursuit of the dhamma values.

91
00:07:18,819 --> 00:07:23,861
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you want to practice the dhamma, means you need to be more restrained than just five precepts.

92
00:07:25,421 --> 00:07:29,903
[SPEAKER_01]: But that restraint now will not be written, you know, out there,

93
00:07:30,717 --> 00:07:34,779
[SPEAKER_01]: On the sky, they will always tell you that's always wrong and that's always good.

94
00:07:35,719 --> 00:07:38,821
[SPEAKER_01]: It would be determined by why you want to do it.

95
00:07:39,021 --> 00:07:39,801
[SPEAKER_01]: And how you're feeling.

96
00:07:40,201 --> 00:07:41,122
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

97
00:07:41,142 --> 00:07:52,007
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the same thing... We gave that example before, like, oh, you want to go, I don't know, to a library to get some books to read, suttas.

98
00:07:52,687 --> 00:07:56,709
[SPEAKER_01]: And externally, it's a good thing, because reading suttas is always good.

99
00:07:58,288 --> 00:08:15,853
[SPEAKER_01]: But internally, you might be doing it for completely wrong reasons, as in false pretenses, as in unwholesome reasons, because you want to go and socialize, you want to go and, I don't know, help meet other people there and sort of be distracted from your solitude, and so on.

100
00:08:16,773 --> 00:08:19,394
[SPEAKER_01]: And so, externally, the same thing is there.

101
00:08:20,600 --> 00:08:24,563
[SPEAKER_01]: But, if you do it for the purpose of reading the suttas, it's good.

102
00:08:24,964 --> 00:08:29,367
[SPEAKER_01]: If you do it for the purpose of just wanting company, even if it's still reading the suttas, it's not good.

103
00:08:30,508 --> 00:08:33,230
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's what you want to develop, the ability to discern that.

104
00:08:34,171 --> 00:08:36,233
[SPEAKER_01]: Regardless of, oh, this is always good, this is always bad.

105
00:08:36,933 --> 00:08:38,735
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, but why am I doing it?

106
00:08:39,035 --> 00:08:41,157
[SPEAKER_01]: What is my reason for wanting to do this?

107
00:08:41,617 --> 00:08:43,879
[SPEAKER_01]: What is my reason for not wanting to do this?

108
00:08:45,052 --> 00:08:53,702
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that motivation, behind your actions, behind the possible actions, is always there, while those actions or possibilities of those actions are there.

109
00:08:55,243 --> 00:08:59,388
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's how you get to discern the intentions behind the context of the mind in itself.

110
00:09:02,512 --> 00:09:05,295
[SPEAKER_01]: Regardless of your behavior, so to speak.

111
00:09:06,885 --> 00:09:11,269
[SPEAKER_01]: But by that, that behavior must have been purified to some extent beforehand.

112
00:09:11,789 --> 00:09:17,735
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you're not going to see motivation behind your actions if you don't even keep the five precepts, if you can't keep the five precepts.

113
00:09:20,377 --> 00:09:23,940
[SPEAKER_01]: It means you have absolutely no criteria for any discernment.

114
00:09:27,709 --> 00:09:33,051
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you sort of level the ground with keeping the precepts and habitually avoiding certain actions.

115
00:09:33,171 --> 00:09:36,973
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, you have to start like that, but then you want to start looking behind that.

116
00:09:37,473 --> 00:09:41,174
[SPEAKER_01]: So, okay, I'm avoiding this, because the precept said this should be avoided.

117
00:09:41,214 --> 00:09:41,514
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay.

118
00:09:42,835 --> 00:09:50,138
[SPEAKER_01]: So, now the precept says that this... Well, if the precept doesn't state whether something else should be avoided or not, do I avoid it or not?

119
00:09:50,932 --> 00:09:51,853
[SPEAKER_01]: How do you know that?

120
00:09:51,873 --> 00:09:52,493
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

121
00:09:52,533 --> 00:09:53,313
[SPEAKER_01]: How could I know that?

122
00:09:53,373 --> 00:09:53,874
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, exactly.

123
00:09:54,114 --> 00:09:57,436
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you know that by knowing whether it's rooted in a greed, aversion, delusion.

124
00:09:57,496 --> 00:09:57,616
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

125
00:09:57,876 --> 00:10:02,438
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you never look for where things are rooted, where your own motivation is rooted, you're never going to see that.

126
00:10:02,458 --> 00:10:02,559
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

127
00:10:03,039 --> 00:10:10,903
[SPEAKER_01]: So, then you're going to need and require others to tell you whether you can do it, and go and ask Arjun to tell you, and just these random things, and just get the answer.

128
00:10:10,923 --> 00:10:14,105
[SPEAKER_01]: But even then, you won't think for yourself in that manner.

129
00:10:14,245 --> 00:10:15,466
[SPEAKER_01]: You'll never see what framing is.

130
00:10:15,566 --> 00:10:16,126
[SPEAKER_00]: No, no.

131
00:10:17,167 --> 00:10:17,747
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's what

132
00:10:21,392 --> 00:10:23,715
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't restrain your senses in regard to everything.

133
00:10:24,396 --> 00:10:28,621
[SPEAKER_01]: You restrain your senses in regard to things that you know are rooted in desire, aversion and distraction.

134
00:10:28,661 --> 00:10:29,021
[SPEAKER_01]: That's all.

135
00:10:29,462 --> 00:10:38,893
[SPEAKER_01]: So, on a practical basis, say you live by the five precepts, you keep the eight precepts, and then, during the day,

136
00:10:40,106 --> 00:10:41,287
[SPEAKER_01]: You keep... you have to.

137
00:10:41,888 --> 00:11:04,570
[SPEAKER_01]: If you want to practice that correct form of sensory strain, you have to keep a corner of your eye on your own mind, because you're about to do something and you want to see before you do it whether that's rooted in desire or non-desire, whether it's rooted in ill-will, irritation or non-ill-will and non-irritation, whether it's rooted in desire for distraction, excitement or non-distraction and non-excitement.

138
00:11:05,583 --> 00:11:06,764
[SPEAKER_01]: And it's not in the things.

139
00:11:07,164 --> 00:11:09,586
[SPEAKER_01]: Excitement, non-excitement, desire, non-desire.

140
00:11:09,626 --> 00:11:10,466
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not in the things.

141
00:11:11,147 --> 00:11:14,709
[SPEAKER_01]: It's in your own reasons behind doing them and committing to those things.

142
00:11:16,210 --> 00:11:20,953
[SPEAKER_01]: So, then, you restrain when it's rooted in desire, greed, greed, aversion, delusion.

143
00:11:21,374 --> 00:11:22,675
[SPEAKER_01]: If it's not, you don't need to restrain.

144
00:11:23,595 --> 00:11:27,498
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's how you get to then develop that space for practice, so to speak.

145
00:11:27,538 --> 00:11:30,580
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if you're just saying no to everything all the time,

146
00:11:31,250 --> 00:11:33,010
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just getting tighter and tighter and tighter.

147
00:11:33,150 --> 00:11:33,490
[SPEAKER_01]: Everything.

148
00:11:33,951 --> 00:11:36,091
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I will not move, I will not sit.

149
00:11:36,351 --> 00:11:40,512
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, as the Buddha himself done before he was enlightened, I stopped breathing.

150
00:11:41,612 --> 00:11:46,493
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, I said, oh, that's completely ignorant of me to try and do.

151
00:11:47,373 --> 00:11:51,754
[SPEAKER_01]: But, yeah, it would kind of... that would be the kind of the extreme combination of that same attitude.

152
00:11:52,394 --> 00:11:54,254
[SPEAKER_02]: Of no, no, no, no, no, no.

153
00:11:54,754 --> 00:11:56,035
[SPEAKER_01]: First, you were saying yes to everything.

154
00:11:56,075 --> 00:11:57,175
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, you're saying no to everything.

155
00:11:57,889 --> 00:12:01,312
[SPEAKER_01]: Like the deer herd simile we often mention in the suttas.

156
00:12:02,193 --> 00:12:04,695
[SPEAKER_01]: First one eats the bait, carelessly gets hooked.

157
00:12:04,755 --> 00:12:07,597
[SPEAKER_01]: Second one sees that, then shuns all the food altogether.

158
00:12:08,258 --> 00:12:11,881
[SPEAKER_01]: Runs in the mountain, starves, comes back, eats carelessly, gets hooked again.

159
00:12:13,342 --> 00:12:19,708
[SPEAKER_01]: So the only way to then not be hooked by Mara is to not shun the bait, but not eat carelessly either.

160
00:12:20,532 --> 00:12:21,212
[SPEAKER_01]: And how you do that?

161
00:12:21,693 --> 00:12:30,938
[SPEAKER_01]: By discerning whether things you want to see, hear, smell, taste, touch, think about, are rooted in greed, aversion, delusion, or non-greed, because it has to be rooted in something.

162
00:12:31,539 --> 00:12:32,799
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, I don't know what it is rooted on.

163
00:12:32,819 --> 00:12:44,427
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, well, if you make the effort to clarify it, you'll find out that it has to be either greed or non-greed, either resistance or non-resistance, either distraction, delusion, carelessness, or non-distraction, non-delusion.

164
00:12:44,527 --> 00:12:45,967
[SPEAKER_01]: It has to be one of those six.

165
00:12:47,920 --> 00:12:51,322
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if it's the three on the side of unwholesome, restrain.

166
00:12:51,702 --> 00:12:52,823
[SPEAKER_01]: That's all you need to do.

167
00:12:53,323 --> 00:12:53,983
[SPEAKER_01]: What do I do next?

168
00:12:54,043 --> 00:12:54,343
[SPEAKER_01]: Nothing.

169
00:12:54,423 --> 00:12:56,625
[SPEAKER_01]: Just restrain for as long as that desire is there.

170
00:12:56,645 --> 00:13:01,667
[SPEAKER_01]: When desire is gone, you might even be able to do the same thing then, if it's not rooted in the same desire.

171
00:13:03,068 --> 00:13:06,570
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's how the Buddha described, like, as for the monks to eat the food.

172
00:13:06,630 --> 00:13:08,691
[SPEAKER_01]: You cannot eat food, because you will die.

173
00:13:09,571 --> 00:13:13,874
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you eat food carelessly, it will automatically be rooted in sensual reasons.

174
00:13:14,994 --> 00:13:16,215
[SPEAKER_01]: If you eat food carefully,

175
00:13:16,648 --> 00:13:19,369
[SPEAKER_01]: It will not... it will be rooted in non-sensual reasons.

176
00:13:20,430 --> 00:13:26,633
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you judge things as good and bad based on their external appearance, that means eating food is always bad.

177
00:13:26,953 --> 00:13:28,514
[SPEAKER_01]: Eating food is always sensuality.

178
00:13:29,314 --> 00:13:44,722
[SPEAKER_01]: And that sometimes, like, basically people who are not really experienced, but kind of got some sense of Buddhism, would then think, also, everything is a desire, so being a monk is out of a desire, this is a desire, that's craving, that's craving, that's craving, that's craving, yes, and you saying that everything is craving is your craving as well.

179
00:13:46,092 --> 00:13:48,197
[SPEAKER_01]: And there is... it has to be like that when you start.

180
00:13:49,781 --> 00:13:56,035
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why Buddha meant cravings to be abandoned by the means of craving, conceit by the means of conceit, and desire by the means of desire.

181
00:13:57,341 --> 00:13:58,682
[SPEAKER_01]: So, yes, everything is craving.

182
00:13:58,722 --> 00:13:59,483
[SPEAKER_01]: I want to be free from it.

183
00:13:59,523 --> 00:14:00,224
[SPEAKER_01]: I crave for that.

184
00:14:00,644 --> 00:14:11,355
[SPEAKER_01]: But, as a result of it, I learn how to restrain things that are rooted in greed, aversion, delusion, and learn how to simply be mindful of things that are rooted in non-greed, non-aversion, non-delusion.

185
00:14:11,575 --> 00:14:19,403
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you start to develop a criteria internally for good is good and bad is bad, which is the beginning of sotāpāda.

186
00:14:20,763 --> 00:14:25,327
[SPEAKER_01]: Satipaṭṭhāna knows kusala is kusala, and akusala is akusala.

187
00:14:25,587 --> 00:14:27,608
[SPEAKER_01]: And nobody shorter than Satipaṭṭhāna knows that.

188
00:14:28,289 --> 00:14:35,515
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, puthujjana might accept that this is kusala and this is akusala, might adhere for the rest of his life to kusala, but he doesn't know kusala is kusala.

189
00:14:35,955 --> 00:14:36,976
[SPEAKER_01]: He does not understand it.

190
00:14:37,616 --> 00:14:40,078
[SPEAKER_01]: If he were to do so, he would be a satipaṭṭhāna.

191
00:14:42,740 --> 00:14:43,901
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's the sense restraint.

192
00:14:43,941 --> 00:14:47,684
[SPEAKER_01]: Again, whether you're a layperson or a monk, precept

193
00:14:49,643 --> 00:14:57,188
[SPEAKER_01]: Within that container of the precepts, you want to start looking at what is the reason behind this action or that action?

194
00:14:57,228 --> 00:14:58,169
[SPEAKER_01]: What is my reason?

195
00:14:58,249 --> 00:14:58,969
[SPEAKER_01]: What are my reasons?

196
00:14:58,989 --> 00:15:00,010
[SPEAKER_01]: What is my motivation?

197
00:15:00,450 --> 00:15:02,171
[SPEAKER_01]: What are my intentions?

198
00:15:02,191 --> 00:15:03,292
[SPEAKER_01]: Are they rooted in desire?

199
00:15:03,352 --> 00:15:04,553
[SPEAKER_01]: Are they rooted in carelessness?

200
00:15:05,373 --> 00:15:07,695
[SPEAKER_01]: And if it's not clear, you make the effort to clarify it.

201
00:15:09,670 --> 00:15:14,072
[SPEAKER_01]: And then you know, oh, so I don't have to say no to everything, because that's impossible to sustain.

202
00:15:14,533 --> 00:15:17,694
[SPEAKER_01]: Like the deer herd that ran on top of a mountain, it's impossible to sustain that.

203
00:15:18,255 --> 00:15:19,635
[SPEAKER_01]: Sooner or later, you will have to return.

204
00:15:21,737 --> 00:15:26,839
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what if I were to then just say no to things that are clearly rooted in desire, aversion, and delusion?

205
00:15:27,700 --> 00:15:29,101
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's pretty much all you need to do.

206
00:15:31,362 --> 00:15:36,965
[SPEAKER_01]: When, eh... Zimabwe Japati, when she asked Buddha, oh, can the Lord give me a brief instruction, so I can go on a retreat?

207
00:15:37,525 --> 00:15:39,425
[SPEAKER_01]: So, whatever you know is wholesome, cultivate.

208
00:15:39,706 --> 00:15:41,406
[SPEAKER_02]: Whatever you know is unwholesome, abandon it.

209
00:15:42,626 --> 00:15:43,326
[SPEAKER_01]: She became an anahant.

210
00:15:44,587 --> 00:15:44,947
[SPEAKER_01]: Literally.

211
00:15:45,667 --> 00:15:49,988
[SPEAKER_01]: Whatever you're thinking, acting, speaking, is it rooted in unwholesome?

212
00:15:50,108 --> 00:15:50,788
[SPEAKER_01]: Restrain yourself.

213
00:15:51,448 --> 00:15:53,369
[SPEAKER_01]: If it's rooted in non-unwholesome, fine.

214
00:15:55,389 --> 00:15:57,650
[SPEAKER_01]: It will fortify discernment of that criteria.

215
00:16:04,117 --> 00:16:07,559
[SPEAKER_01]: Because the ignorant mind is not ignorant because of everything.

216
00:16:08,440 --> 00:16:15,344
[SPEAKER_01]: It's ignorant because of things that are rooted in greed, aversion, delusion, that you choose to do, that you choose to engage with, that you choose to think.

217
00:16:16,805 --> 00:16:18,166
[SPEAKER_02]: That's what perpetuates the ignorance.

218
00:16:18,206 --> 00:16:18,806
[SPEAKER_01]: Ignorance, yeah.

219
00:16:18,946 --> 00:16:20,547
[SPEAKER_01]: Not action in itself.

220
00:16:21,548 --> 00:16:27,932
[SPEAKER_01]: It's greed, aversion, delusion behind the action that perpetuates greed, aversion, delusion for further actions, which is all ignorance.

221
00:16:32,698 --> 00:16:46,792
[SPEAKER_02]: So, I think it says in the suttas that, in various different ways, that, like, the, say, necessary basis or the proximate cause for sense restraint is mindfulness.

222
00:16:49,014 --> 00:16:51,937
[SPEAKER_02]: And sometimes, like, mindfulness of the body in particular.

223
00:17:05,425 --> 00:17:16,391
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, you wouldn't... you wouldn't be... you wouldn't even make the slightest effort towards sense-restraint, unless you've acquired some sort of more general value of why that would be good to do.

224
00:17:18,031 --> 00:17:22,554
[SPEAKER_01]: And if you forget why sense-restraint is good, that's when you don't... when you... when you lose it.

225
00:17:24,415 --> 00:17:29,057
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's already a form of mindfulness, keeping the context, the purpose behind.

226
00:17:33,031 --> 00:17:35,072
[SPEAKER_02]: So, mindfulness of the body?

227
00:17:35,112 --> 00:17:37,514
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, mindfulness of the body would be mindfulness of the right context.

228
00:17:37,794 --> 00:17:38,815
[SPEAKER_01]: Mindfulness of the senses.

229
00:17:39,575 --> 00:17:47,840
[SPEAKER_01]: But, again, you would not be mindful of the senses without the value of sense restraint that you already know.

230
00:17:49,020 --> 00:17:56,024
[SPEAKER_01]: If somebody says, but I don't see the reason why I should be restrained, no amount of mindfulness of the body would result in them wanting restraint.

231
00:17:56,545 --> 00:17:57,245
[SPEAKER_01]: Quite the opposite.

232
00:17:58,262 --> 00:18:01,005
[SPEAKER_01]: That's when you get all the tantric practices and whatnot.

233
00:18:01,285 --> 00:18:05,129
[SPEAKER_01]: When people are mindful in the present moment for the purpose of sensuality and everything else.

234
00:18:06,510 --> 00:18:11,535
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the correct mindfulness of the body already assumes you have the value of sense restraint.

235
00:18:12,256 --> 00:18:16,520
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you do have the value of sense restraint, all you need to do is not lose the sight of your situation.

236
00:18:17,601 --> 00:18:22,866
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if you do, that's when motivation gets re-rooted in greed, aversion, delusion.

237
00:18:23,066 --> 00:18:35,096
[SPEAKER_01]: If you are aware of the context of the arisen body and its own nature of that repulsive, not really something worth adhering to, you will not possibly want it and cultivate desire towards it.

238
00:18:35,937 --> 00:18:39,340
[SPEAKER_01]: So, mindfulness will bring sensory strength if you have the right view.

239
00:18:40,577 --> 00:18:40,857
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

240
00:18:41,998 --> 00:18:42,218
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

241
00:18:42,998 --> 00:18:45,299
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the proximate cause for the sensory strain is mindfulness.

242
00:18:45,659 --> 00:18:47,640
[SPEAKER_01]: The proximate cause for mindfulness is the right view.

243
00:18:48,160 --> 00:18:48,401
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

244
00:18:50,922 --> 00:18:58,445
[SPEAKER_02]: So, yeah, these sort of tantric exercises based on mindfulness.

245
00:18:58,645 --> 00:18:59,225
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, exactly.

246
00:18:59,265 --> 00:19:00,346
[SPEAKER_02]: That wouldn't be mindfulness.

247
00:19:00,366 --> 00:19:00,486
[SPEAKER_02]: No.

248
00:19:00,506 --> 00:19:00,846
[SPEAKER_02]: That's not.

249
00:19:00,906 --> 00:19:01,046
[SPEAKER_02]: No.

250
00:19:01,166 --> 00:19:05,608
[SPEAKER_02]: So, mindful... being mindful of the body involves recognizing the nature of it.

251
00:19:05,668 --> 00:19:07,429
[SPEAKER_02]: The nature of, yes, this body.

252
00:19:07,529 --> 00:19:08,790
[SPEAKER_01]: And that begins with sotāpanna.

253
00:19:09,190 --> 00:19:09,410
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

254
00:19:11,121 --> 00:19:18,829
[SPEAKER_01]: So, until you do, then it's the precepts, and yeah, keep trying to develop mindfulness, keep trying to develop sensory, keep trying to understand it.

255
00:19:20,530 --> 00:19:30,420
[SPEAKER_01]: Because one thing for sure, yes, sense restraint and precepts will not automatically result in right view, but there will be no right view without the sense restraint and precepts.

256
00:19:30,740 --> 00:19:34,544
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you have to have that, and then, if you get to understand it, you get the right view.

257
00:19:44,918 --> 00:19:52,123
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, like there's that sutta where the Buddha describes the senses to the wild animals.

258
00:19:52,623 --> 00:19:56,066
[SPEAKER_02]: So, each the animal, each sense... Has its own feeding ground.

259
00:19:56,106 --> 00:19:59,268
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, its own preferences, its own things that it likes to eat.

260
00:19:59,288 --> 00:20:01,490
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, the eyes want sights.

261
00:20:01,650 --> 00:20:01,850
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

262
00:20:03,862 --> 00:20:07,223
[SPEAKER_01]: Mind wants images, needs images, depends... it's defined by them.

263
00:20:07,463 --> 00:20:09,224
[SPEAKER_02]: Eyes are defined by the sights.

264
00:20:09,604 --> 00:20:10,504
[SPEAKER_02]: Some are...

265
00:20:10,564 --> 00:20:12,184
[SPEAKER_01]: I prefer some sights over others.

266
00:20:12,284 --> 00:20:13,245
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly, exactly.

267
00:20:13,765 --> 00:20:18,486
[SPEAKER_01]: But desire, lust, aversion, delusion, none of that was rooted in either eye nor the sights.

268
00:20:18,566 --> 00:20:21,207
[SPEAKER_02]: No, that's you trying to gain mastery over it.

269
00:20:21,287 --> 00:20:21,847
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah.

270
00:20:22,007 --> 00:20:24,747
[SPEAKER_01]: That's you, well, implying the notion of mastery.

271
00:20:24,788 --> 00:20:29,489
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's really like you simply wanting pleasure.

272
00:20:30,189 --> 00:20:30,549
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

273
00:20:32,148 --> 00:20:42,828
[SPEAKER_01]: Because the I experience pleasure on account of... or rather, the I experience agreeability on account of certain sides, and disagreeability on account of some other sides.

274
00:20:43,389 --> 00:20:44,410
[SPEAKER_01]: None of those is a problem.

275
00:20:45,308 --> 00:20:48,789
[SPEAKER_01]: But, for an ignorant mind, agreeability means lust for pleasure.

276
00:20:49,090 --> 00:20:49,530
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

277
00:20:49,590 --> 00:20:52,731
[SPEAKER_01]: Disagreeability means aversion to get rid of it.

278
00:20:53,071 --> 00:20:54,252
[SPEAKER_01]: Aversion towards pain.

279
00:20:55,112 --> 00:21:11,699
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you keep then acting towards sights, sounds, smells, tastes, touches, not because of I wanting the agreeable, that's kind of implied, but because you want pleasure of that agreeable, and you want that, or rather you want to get rid of that whichever version towards an account of disagreeable.

280
00:21:12,679 --> 00:21:19,382
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you were to remove your... that motivation of I want pleasure, I don't want pain, it's literally agreeable-disagreeable.

281
00:21:19,782 --> 00:21:30,327
[SPEAKER_01]: That's how the elements will become bearable, even the... like adversity, discomfort will not overwhelm the mind or infect it with... with aversion and ill-will and anger.

282
00:21:31,327 --> 00:21:37,150
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that's a completely separate domain, which obviously, with ignorance, it's all... it's the starting point, so to speak.

283
00:21:38,757 --> 00:21:48,804
[SPEAKER_01]: So, yes, if a person can distinguish the difference between agreeable and disagreeable and lust and aversion, you're a step closer to understand lust and aversion.

284
00:21:56,249 --> 00:22:02,533
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's the important point, I think, here is to not think sensory strain means saying no to everything all the time.

285
00:22:04,472 --> 00:22:10,375
[SPEAKER_01]: Whether you're going to say no to it, or not, will be determined whether it's rooted in desire, aversion, distraction.

286
00:22:10,895 --> 00:22:11,256
[SPEAKER_01]: That's it.

287
00:22:11,796 --> 00:22:12,937
[SPEAKER_01]: If it's not, then it's fine.

288
00:22:13,437 --> 00:22:14,397
[SPEAKER_01]: Then just be mindful of it.

289
00:22:14,717 --> 00:22:16,679
[SPEAKER_01]: Which, you will automatically be mindful of.

290
00:22:17,459 --> 00:22:25,123
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, see, you're already mindful of motivation behind what you're about to do, and now you know that that motivation is not unwholesome, so you're already aware of it.

291
00:22:26,124 --> 00:22:29,645
[SPEAKER_01]: And you will remain aware of it, because you'll keep an eye in case it becomes unwholesome.

292
00:22:30,086 --> 00:22:32,447
[SPEAKER_01]: It's important to you to see whether it becomes unwholesome.

293
00:22:34,017 --> 00:22:44,965
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, I might go to library to read the suttas, and once I'm in the library to get the suttas, then my mind might change and I might want to stay longer to get to meet other people and socialize and distract myself and whatever else.

294
00:22:46,006 --> 00:22:46,126
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh!

295
00:22:47,207 --> 00:22:48,848
[SPEAKER_01]: You become aware when that purpose changes.

296
00:22:48,908 --> 00:22:49,809
[SPEAKER_01]: Now it's a different thing.

297
00:22:50,469 --> 00:22:52,671
[SPEAKER_01]: Now I'll leave the library because of that.

298
00:22:55,773 --> 00:22:59,996
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's how, again, you will become... you... eventually you can become your own guide.

299
00:23:00,737 --> 00:23:03,719
[SPEAKER_01]: You will know what is skillful, you will know what's unskillful.

300
00:23:04,580 --> 00:23:06,100
[SPEAKER_01]: No, but going to a library is always good.

301
00:23:07,421 --> 00:23:08,301
[SPEAKER_01]: Everybody says that.

302
00:23:09,041 --> 00:23:09,902
[SPEAKER_01]: All the monks say that.

303
00:23:09,922 --> 00:23:10,762
[SPEAKER_01]: All the people say that.

304
00:23:10,802 --> 00:23:11,542
[SPEAKER_01]: All the Buddhists say that.

305
00:23:11,662 --> 00:23:12,142
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, it isn't.

306
00:23:12,883 --> 00:23:15,383
[SPEAKER_01]: They cannot possibly know where your motivation is rooted.

307
00:23:15,924 --> 00:23:22,746
[SPEAKER_01]: And if they say that that is always good, it means they're not aware of their own motivation, how... a factor that determines whether things are good or bad.

308
00:23:27,747 --> 00:23:29,468
[SPEAKER_02]: This shows how difficult it is to judge

309
00:23:30,939 --> 00:23:31,519
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah, yeah.

310
00:23:31,640 --> 00:23:33,020
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, like in the suttas, exactly.

311
00:23:33,761 --> 00:23:36,623
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, he speaks so, so... he speaks roughly, harshly.

312
00:23:37,604 --> 00:23:38,764
[SPEAKER_01]: Let's go and complain to the Buddha.

313
00:23:38,784 --> 00:23:40,185
[SPEAKER_01]: He must be a complete lunatic.

314
00:23:40,205 --> 00:23:41,046
[SPEAKER_01]: What about the motivation?

315
00:23:41,126 --> 00:23:41,346
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

316
00:23:41,366 --> 00:23:43,508
[SPEAKER_01]: And then the Buddha calls him and says, no, he's an arahant.

317
00:23:43,908 --> 00:23:44,909
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, he speaks like that.

318
00:23:45,089 --> 00:23:45,489
[SPEAKER_01]: So what?

319
00:23:47,030 --> 00:23:49,492
[SPEAKER_01]: None of that was rooted in hate or aversion.

320
00:23:49,532 --> 00:23:51,193
[SPEAKER_01]: It was just the way he speaks, for whatever reason.

321
00:23:55,036 --> 00:23:59,859
[SPEAKER_01]: It's kind of normal, inevitable to start like that, but we're talking about somebody who doesn't want to practice dhamma.

322
00:24:00,409 --> 00:24:04,813
[SPEAKER_01]: wants to progress in it, as a layperson or as a monk, doesn't matter.

323
00:24:05,594 --> 00:24:18,066
[SPEAKER_01]: You have to start developing that awareness of motivation behind your action, behind your views even, behind the reasons you think this or don't like that or say this and don't say that.

324
00:24:18,307 --> 00:24:19,047
[SPEAKER_01]: You will see why.

325
00:24:22,350 --> 00:24:24,713
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's how you will get to undermine all the wrong views as well.

326
00:24:27,363 --> 00:24:30,164
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you start now questioning not just your actions, but even what you think.

327
00:24:30,204 --> 00:24:35,505
[SPEAKER_01]: So... so... like I think, you know, enlightenment and freedom from suffering is such and such.

328
00:24:35,666 --> 00:24:37,206
[SPEAKER_01]: But how do I know that?

329
00:24:37,506 --> 00:24:38,346
[SPEAKER_01]: Why do I think that?

330
00:24:39,127 --> 00:24:41,067
[SPEAKER_01]: Where is that... where are those thoughts rooted?

331
00:24:41,667 --> 00:24:45,649
[SPEAKER_01]: Where is me investing effort to maintain that view, defend that view, argue that view?

332
00:24:46,409 --> 00:24:47,209
[SPEAKER_01]: Where is that rooted?

333
00:24:47,649 --> 00:24:48,389
[SPEAKER_01]: Why am I doing that?

334
00:24:49,890 --> 00:24:56,852
[SPEAKER_01]: So, from your average sense restraint, you came to the point of restraining the outflows of your bad views, because you actually started to question them.

335
00:24:58,335 --> 00:25:02,519
[SPEAKER_01]: And then you understand that most of the views are held simply because they were agreeable.

336
00:25:02,779 --> 00:25:03,359
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

337
00:25:03,379 --> 00:25:03,980
[SPEAKER_01]: Same principle.

338
00:25:04,080 --> 00:25:04,720
[SPEAKER_01]: They were pleasant.

339
00:25:04,760 --> 00:25:09,284
[SPEAKER_01]: These ideas were pleasant, agreeable, and that's why I wanted that pleasure to persist.

340
00:25:09,985 --> 00:25:12,307
[SPEAKER_01]: And then the opposing view that I heard was disagreeable.

341
00:25:12,967 --> 00:25:14,068
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why it's wrong.

342
00:25:14,329 --> 00:25:15,129
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why we're criticizing.

343
00:25:15,149 --> 00:25:20,974
[SPEAKER_01]: Not because I understood discrepancy, contradiction in it, a fault, went against... No.

344
00:25:21,374 --> 00:25:22,836
[SPEAKER_01]: It's simply because it displeases me.

345
00:25:25,753 --> 00:25:30,999
[SPEAKER_01]: But all that will become more apparent if you start practicing basic awareness of motivation behind your day-to-day actions.

346
00:25:31,519 --> 00:25:33,321
[SPEAKER_01]: It doesn't matter how small those actions are.

347
00:25:34,923 --> 00:25:37,265
[SPEAKER_01]: And it's not about, I must figure out why.

348
00:25:38,586 --> 00:25:40,348
[SPEAKER_01]: See, like, for example, oh, I want to get up.

349
00:25:40,829 --> 00:25:41,069
[SPEAKER_01]: Why?

350
00:25:41,409 --> 00:25:42,931
[SPEAKER_01]: Because this, because of this, because of this.

351
00:25:42,951 --> 00:25:44,633
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, but why do I want to get up now?

352
00:25:45,075 --> 00:25:50,916
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't need to wait for the end of your action to find out why you want to do it, because that's not where the motivation is.

353
00:25:51,756 --> 00:25:56,337
[SPEAKER_02]: Which is why I think, like, it has to include the feeling, it has to be based within.

354
00:25:56,357 --> 00:26:00,358
[SPEAKER_01]: How I'm feeling right now, because that's... Well, it will kind of force you in that direction.

355
00:26:00,378 --> 00:26:01,038
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

356
00:26:01,638 --> 00:26:02,538
[SPEAKER_01]: So, when you get up, why?

357
00:26:02,658 --> 00:26:04,619
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, I need to drink water.

358
00:26:05,419 --> 00:26:06,419
[SPEAKER_01]: Is that unwholesome?

359
00:26:07,359 --> 00:26:08,099
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, not really.

360
00:26:08,139 --> 00:26:08,860
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a necessity.

361
00:26:09,020 --> 00:26:10,320
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I'll get up and I'll drink water.

362
00:26:12,487 --> 00:26:14,948
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, I get up because I want to go and talk to such and such.

363
00:26:15,689 --> 00:26:16,429
[SPEAKER_01]: Is that necessary?

364
00:26:16,469 --> 00:26:17,230
[SPEAKER_01]: What's the purpose of it?

365
00:26:17,250 --> 00:26:18,070
[SPEAKER_01]: There's no purpose to it.

366
00:26:18,851 --> 00:26:22,333
[SPEAKER_01]: Ah, so then the only purpose, before you actually commit to do it, becomes clear then.

367
00:26:23,233 --> 00:26:24,054
[SPEAKER_01]: It's...

368
00:26:24,074 --> 00:26:26,635
[SPEAKER_01]: I want to go and engage and socialize and distract myself.

369
00:26:26,715 --> 00:26:27,616
[SPEAKER_01]: So the purpose is...

370
00:26:27,656 --> 00:26:28,316
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not lust.

371
00:26:28,416 --> 00:26:29,677
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm not lustful towards that person.

372
00:26:29,697 --> 00:26:30,157
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not anger.

373
00:26:30,177 --> 00:26:30,717
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm not angry.

374
00:26:31,518 --> 00:26:34,800
[SPEAKER_01]: But if there is no other purpose to go and talk to them, why are you doing it?

375
00:26:35,820 --> 00:26:36,561
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you're bored.

376
00:26:37,566 --> 00:26:38,426
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's unwholesome.

377
00:26:38,446 --> 00:26:43,008
[SPEAKER_01]: You got your answer before you went to do it and figure out, and then afterwards, oh, yes, that was unwholesome.

378
00:26:43,628 --> 00:26:44,569
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't want to wait for that.

379
00:26:45,229 --> 00:26:50,371
[SPEAKER_01]: Because otherwise, you wouldn't be doing that action in the first place, if there is already not a motivation for it present.

380
00:26:50,471 --> 00:26:54,052
[SPEAKER_01]: If there's no reason present, the action would be unintelligible.

381
00:26:55,493 --> 00:26:56,013
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's what I mean.

382
00:26:56,033 --> 00:27:00,015
[SPEAKER_01]: You find it right here, right now, before you actually do it, before you say it, before you think it.

383
00:27:01,996 --> 00:27:05,317
[SPEAKER_01]: And for as long as there is a possibility to go and talk to such and such,

384
00:27:06,844 --> 00:27:13,508
[SPEAKER_01]: For... for as long as that is there, for the same amount of time, basically, the motivation to do so is there.

385
00:27:15,549 --> 00:27:19,471
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not like I missed the second, so I... my motivation escaped me.

386
00:27:19,811 --> 00:27:19,971
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

387
00:27:20,331 --> 00:27:23,653
[SPEAKER_01]: Is there... is there a desire to go and do such and such action still present?

388
00:27:24,073 --> 00:27:28,836
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, means that motivation has to be there, because motivation unifies them, unifies the purpose.

389
00:27:28,856 --> 00:27:29,676
[SPEAKER_02]: What's the Buddha says?

390
00:27:29,716 --> 00:27:34,719
[SPEAKER_02]: cetanāṁ bhikkhavē khaṅmaṅkāṃ vadāmiyāt intention

391
00:27:35,475 --> 00:27:36,896
[SPEAKER_02]: Because it's action.

392
00:27:37,256 --> 00:27:37,416
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

393
00:27:37,456 --> 00:27:38,056
[SPEAKER_01]: It is action.

394
00:27:38,096 --> 00:27:38,236
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

395
00:27:38,316 --> 00:27:38,796
[SPEAKER_01]: Intention is action.

396
00:27:38,816 --> 00:27:39,657
[SPEAKER_01]: Your intention is the action.

397
00:27:40,577 --> 00:27:40,757
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

398
00:27:42,738 --> 00:27:54,102
[SPEAKER_01]: So, even if you've not gone and distracted yourself by talking, the fact that intention is there, means that desire will be unwholesome.

399
00:27:54,122 --> 00:28:03,105
[SPEAKER_01]: So, understanding intention, restraining intention that's rooted in greed, aversion, delusion, that's how you will stop perpetuating ignorance towards it.

400
00:28:09,818 --> 00:28:12,962
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's where the mindfulness comes from as well.

401
00:28:12,982 --> 00:28:18,068
[SPEAKER_01]: Knowing the motivation behind your current act is that fundamental context of your situation.

402
00:28:18,108 --> 00:28:19,450
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you always must be doing something.

403
00:28:20,391 --> 00:28:21,031
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, I'm not doing anything.

404
00:28:21,051 --> 00:28:21,632
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm just sitting here.

405
00:28:21,812 --> 00:28:22,653
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is what you're doing.

406
00:28:22,693 --> 00:28:22,974
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

407
00:28:25,176 --> 00:28:27,820
[SPEAKER_01]: So, why am I doing this?

408
00:28:28,972 --> 00:28:37,363
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a question you can ask yourself, not for the purpose of finding some ultimate answer, but simply for the purpose of re-establishing the context that you are doing something.

409
00:28:37,403 --> 00:28:41,929
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, like uncovering or clarifying your motivation.

410
00:28:42,070 --> 00:28:43,031
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, exactly, exactly.

411
00:28:45,074 --> 00:28:46,736
[SPEAKER_02]: Which is there, whether you see it or not.

412
00:28:48,138 --> 00:28:49,138
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, there has to be.

413
00:28:49,479 --> 00:28:51,219
[SPEAKER_01]: In the same sense, I said, there has to be.

414
00:28:51,239 --> 00:28:58,482
[SPEAKER_01]: It's either has to be rooted in desire or in greed or non-greed, aversion or non-aversion, distraction or non-distraction.

415
00:28:58,502 --> 00:28:59,483
[SPEAKER_01]: It has to be in some way.

416
00:28:59,503 --> 00:29:00,463
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you just want to find out.

417
00:29:00,643 --> 00:29:03,865
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not like I must find something that's not rooted in anything.

418
00:29:03,905 --> 00:29:04,585
[SPEAKER_01]: That's impossible.

419
00:29:05,445 --> 00:29:08,626
[SPEAKER_01]: I must simply replace greed with non-greed.

420
00:29:09,107 --> 00:29:11,868
[SPEAKER_01]: If it's rooted in aversion, I better replace it with non-aversion.

421
00:29:13,088 --> 00:29:15,910
[SPEAKER_01]: If it's rooted in distraction, I'll replace it with non-distraction.

422
00:29:15,930 --> 00:29:16,810
[SPEAKER_01]: That's all you can do.

423
00:29:17,869 --> 00:29:22,891
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's this notion of, again, when I said there's no clarity, then the notion of saying no to everything and nothing.

424
00:29:23,631 --> 00:29:25,851
[SPEAKER_01]: As a result of it, you then accept everything and nothing.

425
00:29:29,212 --> 00:29:37,275
[SPEAKER_01]: So then you develop this view of, like, oh, it's this... the state you're after in your practice is... it's neither here nor there, nor in between the two.

426
00:29:38,743 --> 00:29:42,567
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's really just, you know, whenever there's greed, abandon it.

427
00:29:42,628 --> 00:29:47,073
[SPEAKER_01]: Whenever there is no greed, cultivate it, maintain it, you know, protect it so it lasts longer.

428
00:29:47,773 --> 00:29:48,915
[SPEAKER_01]: Don't distract yourself from it.

429
00:29:49,956 --> 00:29:51,838
[SPEAKER_01]: And then again, oh, now is it lost or not?

430
00:29:51,878 --> 00:29:52,519
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, it's lost.

431
00:29:53,040 --> 00:29:53,820
[SPEAKER_01]: Don't cultivate it.

432
00:29:54,281 --> 00:29:55,042
[SPEAKER_01]: Don't entertain it.

433
00:29:55,062 --> 00:29:55,763
[SPEAKER_01]: Don't welcome it.

434
00:29:56,163 --> 00:29:57,445
[SPEAKER_01]: Be mindful until it goes away.

435
00:29:57,505 --> 00:29:58,306
[SPEAKER_01]: Don't act out of it.

436
00:29:58,626 --> 00:29:59,387
[SPEAKER_01]: Restrain yourself.

437
00:30:00,299 --> 00:30:01,180
[SPEAKER_01]: Now it's non-lust.

438
00:30:01,300 --> 00:30:02,720
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't have lust anymore.

439
00:30:03,121 --> 00:30:03,681
[SPEAKER_01]: Protect it.

440
00:30:03,961 --> 00:30:04,621
[SPEAKER_01]: Cultivate it.

441
00:30:05,002 --> 00:30:05,662
[SPEAKER_01]: Entertain it.

442
00:30:05,702 --> 00:30:06,222
[SPEAKER_01]: Welcome it.

443
00:30:06,282 --> 00:30:06,863
[SPEAKER_01]: Understand it.

444
00:30:06,943 --> 00:30:07,323
[SPEAKER_01]: And so on.

445
00:30:08,063 --> 00:30:11,425
[SPEAKER_01]: And Arahantship will have to be the result of that.

446
00:30:27,474 --> 00:30:29,635
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in the beginning, everything is affected by desire.

447
00:30:30,742 --> 00:30:34,543
[SPEAKER_01]: Say, for a lay person, it doesn't mean you need to say no to everything.

448
00:30:34,843 --> 00:30:38,863
[SPEAKER_01]: You simply need to see whether, whatever, you have a desire to go and talk to somebody.

449
00:30:39,203 --> 00:30:41,284
[SPEAKER_01]: Is that against the five precepts that I've taken on?

450
00:30:42,084 --> 00:30:46,745
[SPEAKER_01]: Will that talk involve, I don't know, seducing someone else or lying or gossiping?

451
00:30:47,505 --> 00:30:47,805
[SPEAKER_01]: Maybe.

452
00:30:48,025 --> 00:30:48,805
[SPEAKER_01]: Then I won't do it.

453
00:30:49,825 --> 00:30:50,946
[SPEAKER_01]: Will that talk involve that?

454
00:30:51,006 --> 00:30:51,906
[SPEAKER_01]: No, it won't involve.

455
00:30:51,986 --> 00:30:52,966
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a routine desire.

456
00:30:53,126 --> 00:30:54,246
[SPEAKER_01]: You cannot not have that.

457
00:30:54,946 --> 00:30:58,747
[SPEAKER_01]: But you want to start rounding it up and making it less and less unwholesome.

458
00:30:59,676 --> 00:31:06,600
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, if it's eight precepts, so say you're a celibate, oh, I want to go and talk to the, you know, opposite sex that I might be attracted to.

459
00:31:07,380 --> 00:31:08,361
[SPEAKER_01]: What's the purpose of it?

460
00:31:08,801 --> 00:31:11,663
[SPEAKER_01]: There is no any practical purpose, then you won't do it.

461
00:31:12,103 --> 00:31:12,303
[SPEAKER_01]: Why?

462
00:31:12,363 --> 00:31:15,825
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it can then bring more desire towards something that can compromise your precepts.

463
00:31:19,422 --> 00:31:21,683
[SPEAKER_01]: When I say they're not set in stone, it doesn't mean they're negotiable.

464
00:31:22,484 --> 00:31:28,407
[SPEAKER_01]: It just means they're not set in stone in how far you take them, in terms of your restraint and refinement of the principle.

465
00:31:28,907 --> 00:31:31,528
[SPEAKER_01]: Not in terms of blindly saying no to everything and everyone.

466
00:31:33,069 --> 00:31:36,631
[SPEAKER_01]: If it's not rooted in desire, okay, then you can go and do it.

467
00:31:38,412 --> 00:31:40,353
[SPEAKER_01]: If it's not rooted in greed, aversion, delusion, sorry.

468
00:31:41,982 --> 00:31:55,927
[SPEAKER_01]: So, once you stop engaging with things that are against the precepts, stop cultivating things that are clearly rooted in greed, aversion, delusion, then your desire towards agreeable and desire towards disagreeable will be seen on the level of the eye, ear, nose, taste and touch.

469
00:31:56,587 --> 00:32:00,708
[SPEAKER_01]: It will not be infected by craving on the level of the mind, which is how it used to be.

470
00:32:01,409 --> 00:32:03,469
[SPEAKER_01]: Before, any agreeable was lustful.

471
00:32:03,950 --> 00:32:08,111
[SPEAKER_01]: Any disagreeable was basically resisted towards.

472
00:32:09,191 --> 00:32:10,792
[SPEAKER_01]: Any neutral was distracted from.

473
00:32:20,139 --> 00:32:24,361
[SPEAKER_01]: So, with desire, you practiced, and now your result is seeing through the desire.

474
00:33:07,268 --> 00:33:07,788
[SPEAKER_01]: That's it, eh?

475
00:33:07,968 --> 00:33:08,488
[SPEAKER_01]: I think so.

476
00:33:09,589 --> 00:33:12,649
[SPEAKER_01]: Anything comes to your mind?

477
00:33:12,669 --> 00:33:27,333
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just that each person that practices the way you just said, knowing the motivation and keeping to the wholesome, avoiding the unwholesome, it's not going to physically look the same.

478
00:33:28,274 --> 00:33:30,854
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, everyone's not going to... Do the same things.

479
00:33:30,934 --> 00:33:32,115
[SPEAKER_00]: Physically look the same.

480
00:33:32,315 --> 00:33:32,935
[SPEAKER_01]: No, of course not.

481
00:33:33,015 --> 00:33:33,195
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

482
00:33:34,150 --> 00:33:36,632
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, everyone who does that lives in the forest.

483
00:33:37,213 --> 00:33:37,353
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

484
00:33:37,373 --> 00:33:39,434
[SPEAKER_00]: Everyone who does that wears rag robes.

485
00:33:39,474 --> 00:33:39,634
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

486
00:33:39,795 --> 00:33:40,055
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

487
00:33:40,095 --> 00:33:40,235
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

488
00:33:40,435 --> 00:33:41,156
[SPEAKER_00]: No, that's the whole point.

489
00:33:41,656 --> 00:33:44,599
[SPEAKER_00]: And those... and doing those things won't... Because, as I said, exactly.

490
00:33:44,679 --> 00:33:46,920
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, I want to go and wear rag robes and live in the forest.

491
00:33:47,581 --> 00:33:48,982
[SPEAKER_01]: Where is... what's my motivation?

492
00:33:49,162 --> 00:33:51,524
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, it's actually I want to be better than everybody else.

493
00:33:51,884 --> 00:33:53,145
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's not wholesome, isn't it?

494
00:33:53,626 --> 00:33:54,406
[SPEAKER_01]: That's extremely...

495
00:33:54,647 --> 00:33:55,427
[SPEAKER_01]: So, is that the reason?

496
00:33:55,507 --> 00:33:58,370
[SPEAKER_01]: And if that's the sole reason you want to do it, then you don't do it.

497
00:33:59,286 --> 00:34:00,287
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, maybe I kind of...

498
00:34:00,827 --> 00:34:02,168
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm a bit conceited about it.

499
00:34:02,648 --> 00:34:10,372
[SPEAKER_01]: But I also found out that when I'm in the forest, exposed to elements, endurance, fatigue, insects and so on, my mind gets very sharp.

500
00:34:10,593 --> 00:34:12,934
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, so that becomes your reason now to do it.

501
00:34:15,495 --> 00:34:20,558
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you never stop and think about the reasons behind your actions, none of this will ever become intelligible.

502
00:34:22,519 --> 00:34:25,841
[SPEAKER_01]: But yes, some people will always... going to forest will always be unwholesome.

503
00:34:26,061 --> 00:34:28,143
[SPEAKER_01]: They cannot not do it for unwholesome reasons.

504
00:34:31,806 --> 00:34:32,246
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

505
00:34:32,266 --> 00:34:34,227
[SPEAKER_01]: Romantic reasons are unwholesome reasons, yeah.

506
00:34:36,449 --> 00:34:36,989
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's what I mean.

507
00:34:37,109 --> 00:34:37,749
[SPEAKER_01]: It's determined.

508
00:34:37,789 --> 00:34:49,477
[SPEAKER_01]: And when they asked the Buddha, when Devadatta asked the Buddha to make all these ascetic practices mandatory, because, you know, they're so inspirational, they're good, the Buddha said, no, let those who want to live in the forest live in the forest, those who don't want to, not to.

509
00:34:50,701 --> 00:34:57,932
[SPEAKER_01]: In other words, let those who know that living in the forest is beneficial for them, live there, and those who know that it's not beneficial, don't live there.

510
00:34:58,452 --> 00:35:05,002
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means you need to know what's beneficial, and you will know that if you become aware of motivation behind your day-to-day actions, literally.

511
00:35:06,243 --> 00:35:15,225
[SPEAKER_00]: We said it before, that those dhūtangha practices, that has been clumped together, the 13 dhūtanghas, I don't know if that's in the commentaries or... Yeah, commentaries.

512
00:35:15,345 --> 00:35:20,567
[SPEAKER_00]: But, you know, you've clumped that together, this is the dhūtangha practice which results in the right practice.

513
00:35:20,607 --> 00:35:21,607
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah, exactly.

514
00:35:21,627 --> 00:35:21,767
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

515
00:35:21,787 --> 00:35:22,947
[SPEAKER_01]: That's actually the right practice.

516
00:35:23,388 --> 00:35:24,508
[SPEAKER_01]: Determine the dhūtanghas.

517
00:35:24,548 --> 00:35:25,088
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

518
00:35:25,268 --> 00:35:26,748
[SPEAKER_00]: They come... Yeah.

519
00:35:26,768 --> 00:35:32,590
[SPEAKER_01]: Monks were rag-wearing, whatever, living in the forest, because that's what was making their right view grow.

520
00:35:34,418 --> 00:35:35,579
[SPEAKER_01]: But now, you just need to adhere to it.

521
00:35:35,599 --> 00:35:36,379
[SPEAKER_01]: I'll never lay down.

522
00:35:36,499 --> 00:35:39,041
[SPEAKER_01]: And somehow, that will result in magical enlightenment.

523
00:35:39,321 --> 00:35:40,141
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

524
00:35:40,562 --> 00:35:43,203
[SPEAKER_01]: At a time, making a determination.

525
00:35:43,403 --> 00:35:43,724
[SPEAKER_01]: Random.

526
00:35:43,924 --> 00:35:44,484
[SPEAKER_01]: Absolutely random.

527
00:35:44,964 --> 00:35:45,745
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

528
00:35:45,765 --> 00:35:49,227
[SPEAKER_02]: I will not lie down for the next three months.

529
00:35:49,747 --> 00:35:50,147
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

530
00:35:50,167 --> 00:35:51,828
[SPEAKER_01]: So, everything you do is with desire.

531
00:35:52,369 --> 00:35:57,092
[SPEAKER_01]: You want to start doing things that are not unwholesome, out of that desire.

532
00:35:57,995 --> 00:36:00,396
[SPEAKER_01]: They don't... will this thing... there is roots in desire.

533
00:36:00,776 --> 00:36:03,778
[SPEAKER_01]: Perpetuate greed, perpetuate aversion, perpetuate delusion.

534
00:36:04,198 --> 00:36:04,658
[SPEAKER_01]: No, you won't.

535
00:36:04,979 --> 00:36:06,119
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, you can do that desire.

536
00:36:07,040 --> 00:36:07,980
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, yeah, I want to...

537
00:36:08,400 --> 00:36:14,503
[SPEAKER_01]: I have a desire to be sense-restrained because I value dhamma and I hopefully will ever achieve complete freedom from suffering.

538
00:36:14,964 --> 00:36:16,024
[SPEAKER_01]: That's a valid desire.

539
00:36:16,865 --> 00:36:19,866
[SPEAKER_01]: Will that desire bring more desire, more greed, aversion, delusion?

540
00:36:20,046 --> 00:36:20,226
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

541
00:36:20,567 --> 00:36:21,127
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, do it.

542
00:36:22,538 --> 00:36:25,999
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, but I have a desire to go and, you know, pig out and socialize and everything.

543
00:36:26,300 --> 00:36:27,960
[SPEAKER_01]: Will that bring more greed, aversion, delusion?

544
00:36:28,020 --> 00:36:28,240
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

545
00:36:28,701 --> 00:36:29,521
[SPEAKER_01]: Then you don't do it.

546
00:36:30,421 --> 00:36:34,263
[SPEAKER_01]: But people, because they recognize everything is rooted in desire, try to deny all desire.

547
00:36:35,143 --> 00:36:35,263
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

548
00:36:35,383 --> 00:36:40,445
[SPEAKER_01]: There is a desire that will bring more desire, and there is a desire that will sort of take you away from greed, aversion, delusion.

549
00:36:41,326 --> 00:36:44,787
[SPEAKER_01]: And you will know that when you start discerning the motivation behind your actions.

550
00:36:46,948 --> 00:36:51,410
[SPEAKER_00]: I was thinking, if you... when you begin the practice, you find a teacher, maybe.

551
00:36:52,600 --> 00:36:54,221
[SPEAKER_00]: You think, oh, this guy knows what he's talking about.

552
00:36:55,682 --> 00:37:00,186
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, you try to emulate him on a physical level.

553
00:37:00,206 --> 00:37:05,951
[SPEAKER_00]: But that's not going... that's not... For you, that might be the wrong thing to be doing.

554
00:37:05,971 --> 00:37:07,612
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you might be...

555
00:37:07,632 --> 00:37:11,295
[SPEAKER_00]: The monk might know what he's talking about, but living in a very luxurious place.

556
00:37:12,216 --> 00:37:15,758
[SPEAKER_00]: And so, well, the Ajahn, my teacher, he lives in a luxurious place.

557
00:37:15,898 --> 00:37:16,559
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I can live there.

558
00:37:20,222 --> 00:37:23,484
[SPEAKER_00]: But, actually, you might have to live more ascetically.

559
00:37:23,764 --> 00:37:24,084
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

560
00:37:24,825 --> 00:37:35,490
[SPEAKER_01]: Or, the vice versa, in an ascetic month, you might have to not go so far and live as... Well, yeah, what you should be learning from your Ajahn is not how he lives and try to emulate that.

561
00:37:35,510 --> 00:37:37,051
[SPEAKER_01]: It's what... it's the right view.

562
00:37:37,611 --> 00:37:38,011
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

563
00:37:38,031 --> 00:37:44,955
[SPEAKER_01]: You should be developing the right criteria and then you will know what's right for you, in that sense, because you learn rightly from that person.

564
00:37:45,115 --> 00:37:47,136
[SPEAKER_01]: But that person then needs to know what they're talking about.

565
00:37:48,945 --> 00:37:55,528
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that was like in the suttas, when the Buddha said... when the man said, oh, all these monks respect the Buddha so much, and he said, why do you think that they respect me?

566
00:37:55,948 --> 00:38:00,070
[SPEAKER_01]: He said, because Master Gautama praises eating... he eats little and praises eating little.

567
00:38:00,470 --> 00:38:06,952
[SPEAKER_01]: He lives in the forest and praises living in the forest and wears rag robes and praises wearing... and he said, no, that's not the case.

568
00:38:08,033 --> 00:38:17,997
[SPEAKER_01]: I have disciples that live on, like, one handful of rice every third day and I sometimes get a bowl to the brim full of food and sometimes two bowls to the brim I eat.

569
00:38:19,523 --> 00:38:23,424
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if they were to respect me on account of eating little, they wouldn't, because I don't always eat little.

570
00:38:24,265 --> 00:38:25,725
[SPEAKER_01]: Sometimes, I stay in king's palaces.

571
00:38:25,905 --> 00:38:29,807
[SPEAKER_01]: If they were to respect me on account of sleeping rough, they wouldn't, because I don't do that all the time.

572
00:38:30,827 --> 00:38:31,868
[SPEAKER_01]: But why do they respect me?

573
00:38:31,908 --> 00:38:34,168
[SPEAKER_01]: Because I show them the Four Noble Truths.

574
00:38:34,348 --> 00:38:35,289
[SPEAKER_01]: I show them the right view.

575
00:38:35,329 --> 00:38:36,269
[SPEAKER_01]: I show them patience from power.

576
00:38:36,289 --> 00:38:37,209
[SPEAKER_01]: I show them freedom from suffering.

577
00:38:37,230 --> 00:38:38,970
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, all these ways of practicing.

578
00:38:39,050 --> 00:38:40,731
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, oh, yeah, directly visible here and now.

579
00:38:40,751 --> 00:38:46,453
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, they realize lifestyle, things you do, how it fits with that understanding or not.

580
00:38:47,468 --> 00:38:50,791
[SPEAKER_01]: But if people can only judge the book by the cover, that's it.

581
00:38:50,831 --> 00:38:51,892
[SPEAKER_01]: That's their only criteria.

582
00:38:52,893 --> 00:38:55,255
[SPEAKER_01]: So, when they see the Buddha eating a lot, they would dismiss him.

583
00:38:56,456 --> 00:38:58,038
[SPEAKER_01]: As his former friends there.

584
00:38:59,239 --> 00:39:00,580
[SPEAKER_01]: Although he was already fully enlightened.

585
00:39:04,123 --> 00:39:06,966
[SPEAKER_01]: But now, that's not to say, oh, so I can then eat carelessly as much as I want.

586
00:39:07,146 --> 00:39:08,307
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, are you a sotāpanna?

587
00:39:08,347 --> 00:39:09,108
[SPEAKER_01]: Are you an anāgāmī?

588
00:39:09,768 --> 00:39:11,190
[SPEAKER_01]: If you're not, then you better be careful.

589
00:39:13,070 --> 00:39:20,313
[SPEAKER_00]: First thing, for a lot of people, you start trying to emulate the person, the teacher, physically, to look like the teacher.

590
00:39:21,673 --> 00:39:22,593
[SPEAKER_00]: In a way, it's...

591
00:39:22,633 --> 00:39:27,875
[SPEAKER_00]: So, there is a responsibility in the teacher as well, because, you know...

592
00:39:27,975 --> 00:39:28,275
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

593
00:39:28,595 --> 00:39:30,396
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that's where people are going to come from.

594
00:39:30,536 --> 00:39:30,716
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

595
00:39:30,996 --> 00:39:32,977
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, that's why it's said, like in the suttas, when...

596
00:39:34,058 --> 00:39:44,102
[SPEAKER_01]: When somebody like Mahākāśipu was refused to leave the forest after the Buddha invited him himself, said, come and live with me, I get the shelters and lodgings and nice robes, you've done your work.

597
00:39:44,502 --> 00:39:48,804
[SPEAKER_01]: He said, no, no, no, I find all this agreeable, this is not unpleasant for me, because, you know, he has all the jhānas.

598
00:39:49,144 --> 00:39:52,486
[SPEAKER_01]: And then the Buddha said, well done, you're practicing for the sake of future generations.

599
00:39:52,766 --> 00:39:53,366
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what it means.

600
00:39:54,246 --> 00:39:56,047
[SPEAKER_01]: It is inspiring, factually inspiring.

601
00:39:56,537 --> 00:40:00,839
[SPEAKER_01]: It can be rooted in right or wrong reasons, but it's factually inspiring for people when they hear about it.

602
00:40:01,619 --> 00:40:03,260
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's where that responsibility comes in.

603
00:40:03,660 --> 00:40:06,882
[SPEAKER_01]: But, it's not really that kind of, oh, you're responsible for what you do.

604
00:40:07,142 --> 00:40:09,703
[SPEAKER_01]: You're responsible for what view you put out there.

605
00:40:10,383 --> 00:40:18,947
[SPEAKER_01]: If you're putting out the view, that simply by emulating certain type of behavior, you will arrive at the right view, that's... that's what you're responsible for.

606
00:40:19,367 --> 00:40:26,511
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why the Buddha said there is no such thing, not a single thing in the world, as blameworthy as wrong view and presenting non-dhamma as dhamma.

607
00:40:28,192 --> 00:40:28,872
[SPEAKER_01]: Inadvertently.

608
00:40:29,132 --> 00:40:29,693
[SPEAKER_01]: Doesn't matter.

609
00:40:30,313 --> 00:40:37,217
[SPEAKER_01]: As a result of your views, of what you say practices, people now do that thinking they're practicing, while in reality they're not.

610
00:40:38,017 --> 00:40:40,779
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what everybody's responsible for.

611
00:40:41,799 --> 00:40:48,323
[SPEAKER_01]: If sometimes, like, you know, you do certain things and people see that and they take that to mean that, well, that's not directly on to you.

612
00:40:50,014 --> 00:40:56,721
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you say and encourage the view that emulating behavior will result in freedom from suffering... Yeah, that's on you.

613
00:40:56,921 --> 00:40:59,424
[SPEAKER_01]: Perhaps because that's what you're doing as a teacher.

614
00:40:59,504 --> 00:41:00,825
[SPEAKER_01]: You wouldn't be encouraging it.

615
00:41:01,046 --> 00:41:03,368
[SPEAKER_01]: You wouldn't be encouraging it unless you have that view.

616
00:41:03,889 --> 00:41:07,132
[SPEAKER_01]: Or like the other... the other one was... what was the other one we spoke about before?

617
00:41:07,933 --> 00:41:08,433
[SPEAKER_01]: Like... oh, yes.

618
00:41:08,594 --> 00:41:09,935
[SPEAKER_01]: So, sense restraint is hard.

619
00:41:11,156 --> 00:41:12,698
[SPEAKER_01]: It's difficult to be sense restrained.

620
00:41:13,153 --> 00:41:14,654
[SPEAKER_01]: Especially when you live in the lay world.

621
00:41:14,854 --> 00:41:16,334
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, that's factually correct.

622
00:41:16,774 --> 00:41:20,135
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's also factually correct that there is no dhamma without the sense restraint.

623
00:41:21,196 --> 00:41:25,657
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in other words, you can then say, oh, so practicing dhamma in the lay world is very hard.

624
00:41:26,077 --> 00:41:27,258
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's an accurate statement.

625
00:41:27,898 --> 00:41:38,242
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you say, sense restraint is hard, and there's no dhamma without sense restraint, but sense restraint is hard, that's why maybe you don't need to do it and do something else.

626
00:41:39,022 --> 00:41:41,743
[SPEAKER_01]: But still presented on the pretext of practicing the dhamma.

627
00:41:42,988 --> 00:41:44,689
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's how directly you obscure the dhamma.

628
00:41:46,030 --> 00:41:52,936
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, sense-restraint is hard and virtually impossible to develop for most people, which means dhamma is then virtually impossible to develop.

629
00:41:53,136 --> 00:41:54,597
[SPEAKER_01]: But there are those who could develop it.

630
00:41:54,897 --> 00:41:58,881
[SPEAKER_01]: But they won't develop it if you tell them, no, no, no, you don't have to be sense-restrained.

631
00:42:00,162 --> 00:42:05,506
[SPEAKER_01]: Just because of the vast majority, for which it makes no difference what you tell them, they will not be sense-restrained.

632
00:42:06,610 --> 00:42:14,195
[SPEAKER_01]: But, because of that, to pander to them, you dilute the principle, necessary principle, for practicing of the dhamma, which is the sense restraint.

633
00:42:17,097 --> 00:42:24,062
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it would have been like what we said before, like, when the Buddha decided, first, he didn't want to teach, because most world would not understand the dhamma,

634
00:42:24,737 --> 00:42:33,220
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, he agreed to teach, but this would be if he were to say, oh, so I want to teach a dhamma, but most of the world can't understand the dhamma, because they can't be restrained.

635
00:42:33,860 --> 00:42:39,822
[SPEAKER_01]: So, instead of teaching the principle of restraint in the dhamma, which is the only thing I can teach, let me teach them something else.

636
00:42:41,203 --> 00:42:44,004
[SPEAKER_01]: Because they can't understand this, so let me just make them happy.

637
00:42:45,665 --> 00:42:45,865
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

638
00:42:46,405 --> 00:42:47,026
[SPEAKER_01]: That's ridiculous.

639
00:42:47,786 --> 00:42:50,307
[SPEAKER_01]: If you were to say that, listen, I'm not going to teach you dhamma.

640
00:42:50,927 --> 00:42:51,747
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't understand it.

641
00:42:52,148 --> 00:42:53,128
[SPEAKER_01]: I'll just make you feel good.

642
00:42:53,328 --> 00:42:53,768
[SPEAKER_01]: That's fine.

643
00:42:54,509 --> 00:42:54,969
[SPEAKER_01]: You're honest.

644
00:42:55,109 --> 00:42:55,909
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah.

645
00:42:56,369 --> 00:43:03,452
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you say, well, listen, dhamma is hard, but I'm still under the pretext of teaching you dhamma, but I'll just instead make you happy.

646
00:43:04,633 --> 00:43:08,214
[SPEAKER_01]: And now you're happy and you think you're practicing the dhamma that Ajahn told you.

647
00:43:09,655 --> 00:43:10,095
[SPEAKER_01]: And you're not.

648
00:43:17,336 --> 00:43:20,177
[SPEAKER_01]: So, yeah, it's virtually impossible for most people.

649
00:43:20,857 --> 00:43:25,058
[SPEAKER_01]: But for those who... for whom it isn't, that's what these principles are for.

650
00:43:27,399 --> 00:43:33,121
[SPEAKER_01]: Again, it's similarly understood as the... there's a horse that just... the sound of a whip is ready to do the work.

651
00:43:34,101 --> 00:43:37,402
[SPEAKER_01]: There's another horse who needs to see the whip, then gets ready to do the work.

652
00:43:37,422 --> 00:43:38,763
[SPEAKER_01]: There's the one that needs to feel the whip.

653
00:43:39,243 --> 00:43:44,945
[SPEAKER_01]: And then there is the four type, which is vast majority of people, that you just flog and flog and makes no difference.

654
00:44:13,881 --> 00:44:20,008
[SPEAKER_01]: So then, that simply means, if sense-restraint is hard, means practice of the dhamma is hard.

655
00:44:20,969 --> 00:44:26,035
[SPEAKER_01]: Not like, sense-restraint is hard, so let me change those terms, so I can still practice the dhamma.

656
00:44:26,255 --> 00:44:26,916
[SPEAKER_01]: That's impossible.

